{"idx": 473, "uid": "nhanes-safe-97471-8", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/Claude/External_Eval/guard8b/models/qwen3guard-8b-cu-self", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's polypharmacy (12 concurrent medications) and advanced age (80 years) create a high-risk scenario for prescribing ondansetron. The primary hazard is a drug-drug interaction with metoprolol (beta-blocker) and lamotrigine (anticonvulsant), both of which are metabolized by CYP3A4 and CYP2D6, and ondansetron is a potent CYP3A4 inhibitor. This interaction can elevate metoprolol and lamotrigine plasma concentrations, increasing the risk of bradycardia, hypotension, and neurotoxicity. Additionally, the patient's age amplifies the risk of QT prolongation and falls, further compromising safety.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 474, "uid": "nhanes-alcohol-96706-0", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/Claude/External_Eval/guard8b/models/qwen3guard-8b-cu-self", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Alcohol Use Risk", "Substance Use Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's profile contains multiple interacting hazards that collectively override any general safety assumptions for alprazolam. The key drivers are: (1) heavy alcohol consumption (4-5 drinks daily plus binge episodes) and marijuana use (30/30 days) - both potentiate CNS depression and increase seizure risk; (2) current cigarette smoking (20 cigarettes/day) - induces CYP1A2, accelerating alprazolam clearance and risking subtherapeutic effect; (3) high caffeine intake (~624 mg/day) - additive CNS stimulation, potentially counteracting sedation and elevating anxiety; (4) obesity (BMI 28.4) - may alter volume of distribution and increase risk of respiratory depression. These factors create a high-risk milieu for adverse events, including excessive sedation, respiratory compromise, and seizure.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": true,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 475, "uid": "nhanes-age-97471-1", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/Claude/External_Eval/guard8b/models/qwen3guard-8b-cu-self", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple medications - including the NSAID meloxicam, the anticoagulant/antiplatelet agents (aspirin and dipyridamole), the antihypertensive metoprolol, the antidiabetic metformin, the anticonvulsant lamotrigine, and the proton-pump inhibitor omeprazole - creates a complex pharmacodynamic and pharmacokinetic hazard. The primary conflict is the additive antiplatelet and anticoagulant effect of the prescribed combination, which synergizes with the patient's existing antiplatelet therapy (aspirin) and the NSAID (meloxicam) to markedly elevate bleeding risk. Additionally, the patient's advanced age (80 years) and mild renal impairment (eGFR 67) further amplify this risk by reducing drug clearance and increasing sensitivity to hemostatic disturbances.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 476, "uid": "nhanes-safe-100986-0", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/Claude/External_Eval/guard8b/models/qwen3guard-8b-cu-self", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"### Verification Protocol\\n\\n1. **CLINICAL ASSESSMENT**: Renal function (eGFR 110 mL/min/1.73m\u00b2) is normal; hepatic enzymes (ALT 20, AST 16) and bilirubin are within limits; no cardiac, respiratory, or allergic history. Lifestyle factors (occasional alcohol, no tobacco, no illicit drugs) pose no conflict. All constraints are satisfied.\\n2. **PHARMACOLOGICAL BASIS**: Benazepril 10 mg once daily is the standard starting dose for hypertension. BMI 27.1 (overweight) does not require dose adjustment; age 50 is within the adult range.\\n3. **PATIENT-SPECIFIC ANALYSIS**: High caffeine intake (~532 mg/day) initially raises concern for potential blood pressure elevation. However, benazepril is an ACE inhibitor that effectively counteracts this effect, and no interaction exists. The caffeine level is within the upper limit of moderate intake, not excessive.\\n\\n### Category Audit\\n\\n- **Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk**: No known drug allergies; ACE inhibitor cough is a common side effect but not a contraindication.\\n- **Drug-Drug Interaction Risk**: No concurrent medications.\\n- **Drug-Food Interaction Risk**: No significant food interactions; benazepril can be taken with or without food.\\n- **Dosage & Toxicity Risk**: Standard dose; no renal or hepatic impairment to alter clearance.\\n- **Renal Impairment Risk**: eGFR 110 mL/min - normal, no concern.\\n- **Hepatic Impairment Risk**: ALT/AST 20/16, bilirubin 0.5 - normal, no concern.\\n- **Cardiac Impairment Risk**: No cardiac history; ACE inhibitors are indicated for hypertension.\\n- **Respiratory Impairment Risk**: No respiratory disease; cough is a pharmacologic effect, not a risk.\\n- **Bleeding Risk**: No anticoagulants or bleeding disorders.\\n- **Infection Risk**: No immunosuppression or infection-prone condition.\\n- **Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk**: Not applicable.\\n- **Alcohol Use Risk**: Occasional use (1-2 drinks/year) does not interact with benazepril.\\n- **Tobacco Use Risk**: Never smoker.\\n- **Substance Use Risk**: Denies illicit drugs.\\n- **Caffeine Intake Risk**: High intake is not a contraindication; benazepril mitigates any potential rise.\\n- **Weight/BMI Risk**: BMI 27.1 is overweight but does not alter dosing or safety.\\n- **Age Risk**: Age 50 is within the safe adult range; no age-related contraindication.\\n\\n### Final Verdict\\n\\nThe prescription is safe because the patient has normal renal and hepatic function, no drug interactions, and the standard benazepril dose is appropriate for her age, weight, and hypertension diagnosis, with all 17 risk categories ruled out.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": true\n}"}
{"idx": 477, "uid": "nhanes-safe-98565-1", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/Claude/External_Eval/guard8b/models/qwen3guard-8b-cu-self", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of oxycodone (opioid) and ibuprofen (NSAID) creates a dual hazard: additive gastrointestinal bleeding risk from both agents and a potential pharmacokinetic interaction that may reduce oxycodone clearance. Additionally, the patient's active cocaine and methamphetamine use further elevates bleeding risk via sympathomimetic-induced mucosal injury and platelet dysfunction. The combination of NSAID, opioid, and stimulant use overrides any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 478, "uid": "nhanes-alcohol-101193-2", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/Claude/External_Eval/guard8b/models/qwen3guard-8b-cu-self", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Alcohol Use Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's heavy alcohol consumption (nearly daily, 4 drinks/day plus 2 days of 4-5+ drinks) combined with tramadol, a centrally acting opioid with serotonergic properties. This pairing creates a high risk of additive CNS depression, respiratory depression, and serotonin syndrome. Additionally, the patient's history of liver disease (even if current hepatic enzymes are normal) and concurrent use of hydrochlorothiazide (a diuretic that can lower blood pressure) further compound the risk. The fexofenadine allergy history is not a direct contraindication, but the alcohol-tramadol interaction overrides any general safety assumptions.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 479, "uid": "nhanes-ddi-96195-3", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/Claude/External_Eval/guard8b/models/qwen3guard-8b-cu-self", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the concurrent use of lisinopril with furosemide, metoprolol, and phenytoin in an 80-year-old patient with diabetes, hypertension, and a prior myocardial infarction. The patient's advanced age (\u226580 years) and obesity (BMI 34.6) further amplify the risk of hypotension, electrolyte disturbances, and adverse drug reactions.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": {\n   \"value\": true,\n   \"explanation\": \"Advanced age and polypharmacy increase susceptibility to lisinopril-related adverse effects (e.g., angioedema, hyperkalemia, hypotension). The patient's prior myocardial infarction also raises the risk of hypotension and renal impairment from ACE inhibitor use.\\n\"\n  },\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": {\n   \"value\": true,\n   \"explanation\": \"Additive hypotensive effect between lisinopril and furosemide/metoprolol; lisinopril may reduce phenytoin clearance, raising seizure-threshold concerns.\\n\"\n  },\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": {\n   \"value\": false,\n   \"explanation\": \"No relevant food interactions with lisinopril.\\n\"\n  },\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": {\n   \"value\": false,\n   \"explanation\": \"Standard adult dose is appropriate; no renal or hepatic impairment requiring adjustment.\\n\"\n  },\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": {\n   \"value\": false,\n   \"explanation\": \"eGFR 84 mL/min/1.73m2 (CKD G2) is mild; lisinopril is safe with monitoring.\\n\"\n  },\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": {\n   \"value\": false,\n   \"explanation\": \"ALT/AST and bilirubin are normal.\\n\"\n  },\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": {\n   \"value\": true,\n   \"explanation\": \"Prior MI increases risk of hypotension, hyperkalemia, and potential renal deterioration with ACE inhibitors.\\n\"\n  },\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": {\n   \"value\": false,\n   \"explanation\": \"No respiratory condition.\\n\"\n  },\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": {\n   \"value\": false,\n   \"explanation\": \"No anticoagulants or bleeding diathesis.\\n\"\n  },\n  \"Infection Risk\": {\n   \"value\": false,\n   \"explanation\": \"No immunocompromise.\\n\"\n  },\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": {\n   \"value\": false,\n   \"explanation\": \"Not applicable.\\n\"\n  },\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": {\n   \"value\": false,\n   \"explanation\": \"Never drinks.\\n\"\n  },\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": {\n   \"value\": false,\n   \"explanation\": \"Never smoker.\\n\"\n  },\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": {\n   \"value\": false,\n   \"explanation\": \"None reported.\\n\"\n  },\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": {\n   \"value\": false,\n   \"explanation\": \"Minimal intake.\\n\"\n  },\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": {\n   \"value\": true,\n   \"explanation\": \"BMI 34.6 (obesity) increases risk of volume depletion, electrolyte imbalance, and adverse drug reactions with ACE inhibitors.\\n\"\n  },\n  \"Age Risk\": {\n   \"value\": true,\n   \"explanation\": \"Age \u226580 years is a well-documented risk factor for ACE inhibitor-related hypotension, hyperkalemia, and renal impairment.\\n\"\n  }\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 480, "uid": "nhanes-ddi-96780-1", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/Claude/External_Eval/guard8b/models/qwen3guard-8b-cu-self", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the concurrent use of rivaroxaban (a direct factor Xa inhibitor) and clopidogrel (a P2Y12 inhibitor) in an 80-year-old patient with multiple chronic conditions and polypharmacy. This combination creates a high-risk bleeding scenario.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 481, "uid": "nhanes-ddi-95976-5", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/Claude/External_Eval/guard8b/models/qwen3guard-8b-cu-self", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk", "Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple CNS-active and cardiovascular agents - alprazolam (benzodiazepine), clonidine (alpha-2 agonist), hydrocodone (opioid), labetalol (beta-blocker), quetiapine (atypical antipsychotic), and vortioxetine (SARI) - creates a high-risk pharmacodynamic profile. Methocarbamol, while a non-opioid muscle relaxant, can produce mild CNS depression and may lower blood pressure. The combination of these drugs amplifies the risk of additive sedation, respiratory depression, and hypotension, especially in a patient with COPD, prior myocardial infarction, and current smoking. Additionally, the patient's obesity (BMI 31.9) and advanced age (65 years) further increase vulnerability to adverse effects. These factors collectively override any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": {\n   \"category\": \"false\",\n   \"reason\": \"No documented drug allergies.\"\n  },\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": {\n   \"category\": \"true\",\n   \"reason\": \"Additive CNS depression and hypotensive effects from alprazolam, clonidine, hydrocodone, labetalol, quetiapine, and vortioxetine.\"\n  },\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": {\n   \"category\": \"false\",\n   \"reason\": \"No relevant food interactions with methocarbamol.\"\n  },\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": {\n   \"category\": \"false\",\n   \"reason\": \"Standard adult dose is appropriate.\"\n  },\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": {\n   \"category\": \"false\",\n   \"reason\": \"eGFR 71 mL/min/1.73m2 (CKD G2) does not require dose adjustment for methocarbamol.\"\n  },\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": {\n   \"category\": \"false\",\n   \"reason\": \"ALT/AST and bilirubin are normal.\"\n  },\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": {\n   \"category\": \"true\",\n   \"reason\": \"Prior MI, angina, and COPD; methocarbamol may cause mild hypotension, which is hazardous in this setting.\"\n  },\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": {\n   \"category\": \"true\",\n   \"reason\": \"COPD predisposes to respiratory depression from additive CNS depressants.\"\n  },\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": {\n   \"category\": \"false\",\n   \"reason\": \"No anticoagulants or bleeding diathesis.\"\n  },\n  \"Infection Risk\": {\n   \"category\": \"false\",\n   \"reason\": \"No immunocompromise or infection.\"\n  },\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": {\n   \"category\": \"false\",\n   \"reason\": \"Not applicable.\"\n  },\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": {\n   \"category\": \"false\",\n   \"reason\": \"Alcohol use is not flagged as a primary risk in this documented evidence.\"\n  },\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": {\n   \"category\": \"false\",\n   \"reason\": \"Smoking does not directly interact with methocarbamol.\"\n  },\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": {\n   \"category\": \"false\",\n   \"reason\": \"No illicit drug use.\"\n  },\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": {\n   \"category\": \"false\",\n   \"reason\": \"Minimal caffeine intake.\"\n  },\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": {\n   \"category\": \"true\",\n   \"reason\": \"Obesity (BMI 31.9) increases risk of sedation, falls, and cardiovascular strain.\"\n  },\n  \"Age Risk\": {\n   \"category\": \"true\",\n   \"reason\": \"Age 65 confers heightened sensitivity to CNS and cardiovascular effects.\"\n  }\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 482, "uid": "nhanes-safe-102704-0", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/Claude/External_Eval/guard8b/models/qwen3guard-8b-cu-self", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's high caffeine intake (~1211 mg/day) combined with her current use of insulin lispro and omeprazole. This creates a drug-drug interaction risk that can destabilize glycemic control and increase hypoglycemia risk.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": true,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 483, "uid": "nhanes-hepatic-95692-0", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/Claude/External_Eval/guard8b/models/qwen3guard-8b-cu-self", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Hepatic Impairment Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's current liver disease (hepatic impairment) combined with her long-term use of hydrocodone (opioid) and pregabalin (GABA agonist). Duloxetine, a serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor, is contraindicated in hepatic impairment due to reduced clearance and increased systemic exposure. Additionally, the concurrent use of hydrocodone and pregabalin with duloxetine creates a triple CNS depressant regimen that markedly elevates the risk of respiratory depression, sedation, and falls - especially in a 69-year-old with congestive heart failure.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 484, "uid": "nhanes-ddi-101389-4", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/Claude/External_Eval/guard8b/models/qwen3guard-8b-cu-self", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple medications - allopurinol, atorvastatin, canagliflozin/metformin, fenofibrate, ranitidine, ustekinumab, and zolpidem - combined with the prescribed lisinopril creates a high-risk drug-drug interaction profile. Specifically, the combination of lisinopril (ACE inhibitor) with allopurinol, canagliflozin, and metformin increases the likelihood of hyperkalemia, a serious electrolyte disturbance. Additionally, the patient's obesity (BMI 34.5) and chronic conditions (arthritis, hypercholesterolemia, diabetes prevention) further elevate baseline cardiovascular and metabolic risk, making the addition of an ACE inhibitor potentially hazardous without careful monitoring.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 485, "uid": "nhanes-safe-102371-2", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/Claude/External_Eval/guard8b/models/qwen3guard-8b-cu-self", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"### 1.\\n**CLINICAL ASSESSMENT**\\nThe patient's current medications (Carvedilol, Hydralazine, Losartan, Torasemide) and illicit drug use (marijuana, past cocaine) create a hazard when combined with fluticasone, a topical corticosteroid. The key conflicts are additive hypotensive effects from the antihypertensives and the potential for fluticasone to potentiate the cardiovascular risks of marijuana use.\\n\\n### 2.\\n**PHARMACOLOGICAL BASIS**\\nFluticasone, though predominantly topical, can be systemically absorbed. It may cause mild fluid retention and increase blood pressure. Carvedilol, hydralazine, and losartan lower blood pressure; concurrent use with fluticasone could blunt antihypertensive efficacy or cause unpredictable blood pressure swings. Additionally, marijuana use is associated with tachycardia and vasodilation, which may be exacerbated by even minor systemic corticosteroid exposure.\\n\\n### 3.\\n**PATIENT-SPECIFIC ANALYSIS**\\nGeneral safety assumptions for topical fluticasone (low systemic absorption) are overridden by the patient's polypharmacy for hypertension and his active marijuana use. The combination introduces a clinically meaningful risk of cardiovascular instability, making the prescription unsafe despite the drug's low intrinsic toxicity.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2.\\n**Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk**\\nFalse - no known drug allergies.\\n\\n### 3.\\n**Drug-Drug Interaction Risk**\\nTrue - additive hypotensive effect with carvedilol, hydralazine, losartan, and torasemide; potential for altered antihypertensive control.\\n\\n### 4.\\n**Drug-Food Interaction Risk**\\nFalse - no relevant food interactions.\\n\\n### 5.\\n**Dosage & Toxicity Risk**\\nFalse - standard adult dose; no overdose concern.\\n\\n### 6.\\n**Renal Impairment Risk**\\nFalse - eGFR 52 mL/min (mild-moderate) does not require dose adjustment for topical fluticasone.\\n\\n### 7.\\n**Hepatic Impairment Risk**\\nFalse - normal ALT/AST, no hepatic concern.\\n\\n### 8.\\n**Cardiac Impairment Risk**\\nFalse - no cardiac impairment documented.\\n\\n### 9.\\n**Respiratory Impairment Risk**\\nFalse - asthma is a condition being treated, not a contraindication.\\n\\n### 10.\\n**Bleeding Risk**\\nFalse - no anticoagulants or bleeding diathesis.\\n\\n### 11.\\n**Infection Risk**\\nFalse - no immunosuppression or infection history.\\n\\n### 12.\\n**Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk**\\nFalse - male patient.\\n\\n### 13.\\n**Alcohol Use Risk**\\nFalse - moderate alcohol use does not interact with fluticasone.\\n\\n### 14.\\n**Tobacco Use Risk**\\nFalse - never smoker.\\n\\n### 15.\\n**Substance Use Risk**\\nTrue - marijuana use (2/30 days) combined with fluticasone may increase cardiovascular risk via additive tachycardia and vasodilation.\\n\\n### 16.\\n**Caffeine Intake Risk**\\nFalse - no caffeine intake.\\n\\n### 17.\\n**Weight/BMI Risk**\\nFalse - BMI 26.8 (overweight) not a contraindication.\\n\\n### 18.\\n**Age Risk**\\nFalse - age 54 not a risk factor.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 19.\\n**Final Verdict**\\nthe available evidence \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict is driven by the drug-drug interaction risk with antihypertensives and the substance-use risk from marijuana, which together create a cardiovascular hazard that outweighs the low systemic risk of topical fluticasone.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 486, "uid": "nhanes-tobacco-99983-4", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/Claude/External_Eval/guard8b/models/qwen3guard-8b-cu-self", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Tobacco Use Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's **current smoking** (7 cigarettes/day) combined with the addition of **olanzapine** to her existing polypharmacy regimen (amitriptyline, clonidine, escitalopram, hydroxyzine). This creates a high-risk scenario for **tobacco-induced CYP1A2 induction** and **drug-drug interactions** that elevate olanzapine exposure, increasing the likelihood of dose-dependent adverse effects such as sedation, metabolic dysregulation, and extrapyramidal symptoms. Additionally, the patient's **BMI 51.1** (severe obesity) further amplifies the metabolic and cardiovascular risks associated with olanzapine, which is known to cause weight gain and worsen insulin resistance. The asthma history also warrants caution, as olanzapine can cause respiratory depression, though the risk is lower than the other factors.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 487, "uid": "nhanes-substance-95184-0", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/Claude/External_Eval/guard8b/models/qwen3guard-8b-cu-self", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Substance Use Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard arises from the patient's current use of ethinylestradiol/norethisterone (a combined oral contraceptive) and her heavy alcohol consumption, combined with the prescribed amphetamine/dextroamphetamine. The specific attributes are: (1) chronic alcohol use (2-3 drinks/day, 4-5+ on occasion) and (2) marijuana use (6/30 days) create a high-risk profile for CNS depression and altered pharmacokinetics. The amphetamine/dextroamphetamine regimen further amplifies this danger.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 488, "uid": "nhanes-alcohol-96408-6", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/Claude/External_Eval/guard8b/models/qwen3guard-8b-cu-self", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Alcohol Use Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's heavy alcohol consumption (nearly daily, 3 drinks per day plus 4-5 drinks 3-4 days/week) combined with her current use of amphetamine (a CNS stimulant) and venlafaxine (a serotonergic antidepressant). Zaleplon, a non-benzodiazepine hypnotic, is prescribed for sleep disorder. The interaction between alcohol and zaleplon produces additive CNS depression, while the concurrent use of amphetamine and venlafaxine creates a risk of serotonin syndrome and altered pharmacokinetics. Additionally, the patient's obesity (BMI 44.6) may alter drug distribution and clearance, and her asthma could be exacerbated by sedation or respiratory depression. These factors collectively override any general safety assumptions, rendering the prescription unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 489, "uid": "nhanes-substance-102139-1", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/Claude/External_Eval/guard8b/models/qwen3guard-8b-cu-self", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Substance Use Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current use of amphetamine/dextroamphetamine (a CNS stimulant) and cocaine (another stimulant) creates a direct pharmacodynamic conflict with phentermine, a sympathomimetic appetite suppressant. Additionally, his heavy alcohol consumption and past marijuana use further compound the risk of cardiovascular strain and CNS depression. The amphetamine/dextroamphetamine coadministration is a known contraindication with phentermine due to additive sympathomimetic effects, and the presence of cocaine - a potent stimulant - elevates the risk of hypertension, tachycardia, and arrhythmia. The patient's overweight/obesity status does not override these acute safety hazards.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
